window.__DIYDB__=window.__DIYDB__||{data:{}};window.__DIYDB__.data["jk"]=[{"id":"jk-brake-line-inspection","title":"Brake Line and Hose Inspection","vehicle":"jk","category":"brakes","subcategory":"lines_hoses","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":220},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","flashlight","10mm and 14mm flare wrench","brake fluid catch pan"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","brake bleeding"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2007-2018"],"tags":["safety-critical","lift","inspection"],"parts":[{"name":"Rough Country stainless brake lines (4-6\" lift, front+rear set)","part_number":"89716","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":130},{"name":"Rough Country rear extended stainless line set","part_number":"89708","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":80},{"name":"MetalCloak JK brake lines (24.5\" pair, Kevlar core)","vendor":"MetalCloak","price_estimate_usd":180},{"name":"Synergy JK extended brake line kit (25.07\")","vendor":"Synergy Manufacturing","price_estimate_usd":220}],"citations":[{"title":"Rough Country 89716 Stainless Brake Lines for JK 4-6\" lift","url":"https://www.amazon.com/Rough-Country-Stainless-2007-2018-89716/dp/B07PZXQMK4"},{"title":"MetalCloak JK Replacement Brake Lines 24.5\"","url":"https://metalcloak.com/jk-wrangler-replacement-brake-lines-pair.html"},{"title":"Synergy JK Extended Brake Line Kit","url":"https://www.synergymfg.com/synergy-jeep-jk-extended-brake-line-kit.html"}],"alternatives":["jk-front-pads-rotors","jk-rear-disc-brakes"],"summary":"Inspect brake lines and hoses every oil change \u2014 cracked rubber, bulges, stretched hoses after a lift, and corroded hardlines all kill braking and can fail without warning.","description":"Brake lines on the JK include hardlines running along the frame and flexible rubber hoses at each corner. Rubber hoses are the failure point. Heat, ozone, road salt, and stretching during full suspension droop all degrade them. A lifted JK puts the front hoses near or past their stock length at full droop \u2014 over time the rubber cracks at the crimp, and on a hard left turn at full articulation a line can tear. You lose half the brake system in seconds.\n\nLook at each flex hose with a strong flashlight. Bend it gently with your fingers. You're looking for cracks in the outer rubber (small ones are normal, deep ones mean replacement), bulges or balloons when the brakes are pressed, dampness or staining at the crimp ends, and any contact with tire, sway bar, or axle in either compression or droop. On hardlines, run a fingernail along the line where it sits in the frame brackets \u2014 that's where road salt traps and rust hides under the clip. Any flaking, scaling, or pinholes means replace.\n\nAfter any lift over 2.5\", upgrade to extended stainless lines. Stock hoses are 22-23\" effective length; aftermarket sets run 24.5\u201325\" with Kevlar or Teflon cores wrapped in stainless braid. Rough Country, MetalCloak, and Synergy all make DOT-approved sets in the $130\u2013$220 range. Stainless lines also give a firmer pedal because they don't balloon under pressure the way old rubber does.\n\nIf your JK is a salt-belt vehicle and the hardlines have visible corrosion, plan a full hardline replacement before the corrosion makes a slow leak. Cunifer (copper-nickel) replacement line is rust-proof, bends by hand, and flares with a standard double-flare tool. Pre-bent line kits exist for the JK; expect a long afternoon to swap them all.","steps":["Park on level ground, chock the rear wheels, and lift one corner at a time with a jack stand under the frame.","Let the suspension droop fully so the flex hose is at maximum extension.","Inspect each flex hose end-to-end with a flashlight. Bend it 90\u00b0 and look for surface cracking.","Check the crimp at each end for dampness, staining, or weeping.","Cycle the steering lock-to-lock and watch for contact with tire, frame, or control arm.","Run your fingernail along every hardline section and inspect each frame bracket for rust or scale.","Press the brake pedal hard while a helper watches each hose for balloon or bulge.","Replace any hose with cracks, bulges, contact wear, or weeping. Replace any hardline section with pinholes or heavy scale.","Bleed the system after any line replacement \u2014 both calipers, longest line first."],"pros":["Catches a failure before it fails","Stainless upgrade gives firmer pedal and survives trail abuse","Cheap insurance \u2014 $130 for the front set","Inspect-only costs nothing"],"cons":["Salt-belt hardlines are a long job if all need replacement","Bleeding takes a helper or a pressure bleeder","Some \"extended\" stainless kits are barely longer than stock \u2014 measure before you buy"]},{"id":"jk-front-pads-rotors","title":"Front Brake Pad and Rotor Replacement","vehicle":"jk","category":"brakes","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1.5,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":100,"high":280},"tools_required":["13mm socket","18mm socket","21mm socket","torque wrench","C-clamp","wire brush","brake cleaner","jack stands"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","wheel removal"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2007-2010","2011-2018"],"tags":["brakes","pads","rotors","safety-critical","maintenance"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar OEM front brake pads","part_number":"68003610AA","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":60},{"name":"Mopar OEM front rotors (pair)","part_number":"52060137AB","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":160},{"name":"Powerstop Z36 Truck and Tow front pad/rotor kit","part_number":"K2901-36","vendor":"Powerstop","price_estimate_usd":220},{"name":"EBC Yellowstuff DP41722R pads (heavy use)","vendor":"EBC","price_estimate_usd":90}],"citations":[{"title":"JK Brake Torque Specs Reference Thread","url":"https://wranglerjkforum.net/threads/jk-brake-torque-specs.28635/"},{"title":"Jeep JK Wrangler Torque Settings Master List","url":"https://www.project-jk.com/jeep-jk-write-ups/jeep-jk-wrangler-torque-settings"}],"alternatives":["jk-rear-disc-brakes","jk-brake-line-inspection"],"summary":"JK front pads run 35,000\u201350,000 miles stock and noticeably less on lifted 35\"-tire builds. Caliper slider bolts torque to 11 ft-lb (delicate \u2014 strip easily), caliper bracket bolts to 100 ft-lb (2011+) or 120 ft-lb (2007\u20132010).","description":"The JK brake setup is straightforward American-iron sliding caliper. Two slider bolts, two bracket bolts, single piston per side, vented front rotors. Where the JK differs from later JLs is in the slider bolt torque \u2014 Chrysler spec is only 11 ft-lb on the 13mm slider bolts. Crank these to \"feels tight\" with a regular ratchet and you'll either strip the caliper bracket threads or snap the bolt. Use the torque wrench.\n\nJK brakes have a reputation for being undersized for the truck, especially once you lift it and add weight. Stock pads and rotors will get you through daily driving without complaint. Add 35\" tires, an aftermarket bumper, and a winch and you'll start feeling the front end push through the brakes on long descents. The fix isn't necessarily aftermarket \"performance\" rotors \u2014 it's a heavier pad compound (Powerstop Z36 or EBC Yellowstuff) and disciplined downshifting on grade.\n\nRotor warp shows up early on JKs that see water crossings. The factory rotors are mid-grade castings and they don't tolerate thermal shock well. Replace when warped \u2014 resurfacing rarely lasts a full pad set. Powerstop's plain-faced rotor kits cost less than OEM Mopar and last as long for street and mild trail use.\n\nThe procedure is almost identical to any sliding-caliper brake job, but JK-specific note: you can swing the caliper up off one slider bolt to swap pads without fully removing it. Only do this if you're not replacing rotors. If the rotor is staying, removing one bolt and swinging the caliper up out of the way speeds the pad swap to 30 minutes per side.\n\nBracket bolt torque is the one to get right. The 2007\u20132010 JK uses 120 ft-lb on the front bracket bolts (18mm); the 2011 and later specs dropped to 100 ft-lb. Use a torque wrench and verify against your year \u2014 the 2011+ spec being lower is because Mopar revised the bracket bolt grade. Over-torquing a 2011+ bracket bolt can stretch it.","steps":["Crack the lug nuts (19mm or 21mm depending on year/wheel), lift the front of the Jeep, support on stands at the frame rails behind the front axle.","Remove front wheels.","Open the brake fluid reservoir cap on the master cylinder. Drape a rag.","Remove the two 13mm caliper slider bolts on the back of the caliper. Slide caliper off, support with a wire hanger.","Compress the caliper piston with a C-clamp using an old pad as a buffer. Move slowly.","Pull old pads from bracket. Note shim orientation.","If replacing rotors: remove the two bracket bolts (18mm \u2014 100 ft-lb on 2011+, 120 ft-lb on 2007\u20132010). Lift bracket off. Pull old rotor.","Wire-brush the hub face clean. Any rust ridges here cause runout.","Install new rotor (light anti-seize on hub mating face, not on the friction surface). Reinstall bracket and torque bolts to year-specific spec.","Install new pads with new anti-rattle clips. Light brake grease on pad backs and slider pins.","Slide caliper over pads onto the bracket. Reinstall slider bolts and torque to 11 ft-lb \u2014 do not exceed.","Reinstall wheels, torque lugs to 95 ft-lb.","Pump brake pedal until firm. Top off reservoir.","Bed in: 8\u201310 moderate stops from 35 mph, then 2\u20133 hard stops from 50 mph, then cool down without dragging."],"pros":["Pad swap without removing caliper bracket (swing-up method) is under an hour","Mopar OEM parts cheap and available; aftermarket options well-supported","Standard sliding caliper \u2014 common tools, well-documented","35K\u201350K pad life on stock setups"],"cons":["Caliper slider bolts (11 ft-lb) easily over-torqued and stripped","Bracket bolt torque differs between 2007\u20132010 and 2011+ \u2014 verify by year","Stock brakes feel undersized on lifted 35\"-tire builds","Rotors warp early on rigs that see water crossings"]},{"id":"jk-rear-disc-brakes-jk","title":"Rear Disc Brake Service","vehicle":"jk","category":"brakes","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":80,"high":220},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","18mm socket","21mm socket for caliper bracket","c-clamp or piston compressor","torque wrench","brake cleaner","wire brush"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","brake bleeding awareness"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2007-2018"],"tags":["brakes","maintenance","safety-critical"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar OEM Rear Pad Set (semi-metallic)","part_number":"68003776AA","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":65},{"name":"Akebono ProACT Ceramic Rear Pads","part_number":"ACT1274","vendor":"Akebono","price_estimate_usd":55},{"name":"PowerStop Z36 Truck/Tow Rear Rotor + Pad Kit","part_number":"K2798","vendor":"PowerStop","price_estimate_usd":180},{"name":"Centric Premium Rear Rotor (each)","part_number":"120.67064","vendor":"Centric","price_estimate_usd":55}],"citations":[{"title":"JK Brake Torque Specs","url":"https://wranglerjkforum.net/threads/jk-brake-torque-specs.28635/"},{"title":"Jeep Wrangler JK Rear Brake Replacement","url":"https://www.jk-forum.com/how-tos/a/jeep-wrangler-jk-how-to-replace-brake-pads-calipers-and-rotors-408088"},{"title":"Mopar OEM JK Brake Parts","url":"https://www.moparonlineparts.com/part-model/jeep-wrangler-jk-brake-pad.html"}],"alternatives":["jk-front-pads-rotors-jk","jk-brake-line-inspection-jk"],"summary":"Rear disc brake service on a JK is a one-hour-per-side job: pull the caliper, compress the piston, swap pads, and re-torque to 77 ft-lbs on the bracket bolts.","description":"The JK runs disc brakes at all four corners from 2007 on, which is one of the upgrades JK owners take for granted until they go back and try to service drums on a TJ. Rear pads on a JK typically last 50,000 to 80,000 miles depending on tire size, towing, and how much you ride the brake in 4-low. Larger tires and aftermarket bumpers shorten that interval because the unsprung weight and total mass climb without a corresponding brake-system upgrade.\n\nThe factory rear pad is semi-metallic. Akebono ProACT ceramics (ACT1274) are the most common upgrade for owners who hate brake dust on the wheels. Ceramics also run cooler in stop-and-go driving but bite slightly softer when cold than semi-metallics. For tow rigs or anyone running 35s and up, PowerStop's Z36 truck-and-tow rear kit (K2798 covers fronts and rears) is the more honest answer than chasing exotic pads on stock-size rotors. You probably don't need slotted-and-drilled rotors for a JK that sees pavement and dirt \u2014 they look the part but the drilled holes are a stress riser, not a performance gain.\n\nInspect the parking brake shoes while you're back there. The JK uses a drum-in-hat parking brake inside the rear rotor, and the shoes get glazed and the adjusters seize on Jeeps that mostly live in 2WD-Auto. If the rotor won't pull off, back off the adjuster through the access slot before forcing it.\n\nTorque the rear caliper guide pin bolts to 22 ft-lbs and the caliper bracket-to-knuckle bolts to 77 ft-lbs. Lug nuts go to 95 ft-lbs in a star pattern. Pump the pedal a dozen times before driving to seat the pad against the rotor \u2014 the first stop with new pads should be at low speed in a parking lot, not on the freeway.","steps":["Loosen rear lug nuts, then jack the rear axle and set jack stands under the axle tubes. Pull the wheel.","Remove the two caliper guide pin bolts (13mm head, hex internal on some years) and lift the caliper off. Hang it from a coil with mechanic's wire \u2014 do not let it dangle on the hose.","Slide the old pads out of the bracket. Note clip and shim orientation; new pads usually include fresh hardware.","Remove the two caliper bracket bolts (21mm) and pull the bracket off so you can remove the rotor.","Pull the rotor straight off. If it's stuck, back off the parking brake adjuster through the access hole, then strike the rotor hat with a deadblow.","Wire-brush the hub face flat. Any rust ridge here will cause runout and pulsation.","Install the new rotor. Reinstall the caliper bracket \u2014 torque to 77 ft-lbs.","Compress the caliper piston with a c-clamp or piston tool. Open the master cylinder cap first and watch the level so it doesn't overflow.","Install new pads with anti-squeal compound on the back of the pad ears, slide the caliper over, and torque guide pin bolts to 22 ft-lbs.","Reinstall the wheel, torque lug nuts to 95 ft-lbs, lower the Jeep, pump the pedal until firm, then bed the pads with 8\u201310 moderate stops from 35 mph."],"pros":["One-hour-per-side job with hand tools","Big improvement in pedal feel after even basic pad-and-rotor refresh","Drum-in-hat parking brake can be inspected at the same time"],"cons":["Parking brake adjusters seize and complicate rotor removal","Stock master cylinder is a known weak point on lifted JKs running 35s+ \u2014 pads alone won't fix soft pedal","Caliper guide pins need fresh grease every service or they bind and chew pads unevenly"]},{"id":"jk-int-steering-wheel-2012-swap","title":"2012 JK Steering Wheel Swap into 2009 JK","vehicle":"jk","category":"interior","subcategory":"steering","type":"swap","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":850,"high":1640},"tools_required":["13mm socket","torque wrench","T20 driver","10mm socket","dental pick or small flathead","panel removal tool"],"skills_required":["airbag handling","basic electrical","patience with bus signaling"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2007-2010"],"tags":["steering-wheel","airbag","clockspring","cruise-control","head-unit","carplay","lin-bus","can-bus","maestro","swc"],"parts":[{"name":"2011\u20132018 JK leather steering wheel","part_number":"1TT67DX9AE","vendor":"Mopar (used)","price_estimate_usd":225},{"name":"2011+ JK driver airbag","part_number":"1GG37DX9AC","vendor":"Mopar (used)","price_estimate_usd":150},{"name":"Clockspring (OEM)","part_number":"68078303AC","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":180},{"name":"Clockspring (aftermarket, fits 2007\u20132012 JK)","part_number":"5156106AD","vendor":"Crown Automotive","price_estimate_usd":100},{"name":"iDatalink Maestro RR2 + HRN-AV-CH1 t-harness","part_number":"ADS-MRR2 + HRN-AV-CH1","vendor":"iDatalink","price_estimate_usd":200},{"name":"PAC SWI-CP5 steering wheel control interface (backup path)","part_number":"SWI-CP5","vendor":"PAC","price_estimate_usd":70},{"name":"Scosche LUSWC (budget SWC interface)","part_number":"LUSWC","vendor":"Scosche","price_estimate_usd":60},{"name":"Alpine i509-WRA-JK 9\" Jeep-specific head unit","part_number":"i509-WRA-JK","vendor":"Alpine","price_estimate_usd":1200},{"name":"Stinger HEIGH10 RB10JK11B 10\" head unit","part_number":"RB10JK11B","vendor":"Stinger","price_estimate_usd":1000},{"name":"Kenwood DMX7706S double-DIN CarPlay/AA","part_number":"DMX7706S","vendor":"Kenwood","price_estimate_usd":450}],"citations":[{"title":"iDatalink install guides","url":"https://www.idatalink.com/support/install-guides"},{"title":"iDatalink Maestro firmware portal","url":"https://www.idatalinkmaestro.com"},{"title":"Crutchfield vehicle-specific harness bundling","url":"https://www.crutchfield.com"},{"title":"JK-Forum: Successful 2010 Jeep Wrangler JK Steering Wheel Upgrade (ESP32 LIN bridge)","url":"https://www.jk-forum.com"},{"title":"Mopar TechAuthority (factory wiring diagrams)","url":"https://www.techauthority.com"},{"title":"BBB Industries free TSB / wiring diagrams","url":"https://www.bbbind.com"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"A 2012 JK steering wheel will physically bolt onto a 2009 JK because both use the same JK platform (2007\u20132018) with the same column splines and 6-pin clockspring connector. Cruise, horn, and airbag carry over cleanly. Audio buttons use LIN bus on 2011+ wheels \u2014 the 2009 cluster can't decode them, so an aftermarket head unit with an interface module (Maestro RR2 or PAC SWI-CP5) is needed for audio button function.","description":"The wheel swap itself is physically straightforward. The hard part is making the audio buttons work \u2014 and that requires understanding which subsystem each button uses.\n\n**How the buttons work.** The 6-pin clockspring connector is identical between 2009 and 2012 JKs. Pin 1 is ground, pin 2 is the LIN bus, pin 3 is fused B+, pins 4 and 6 are S/C Signal 1 and 2, and pin 5 is S/C Switch Ground. What changed in 2011 is *what the buttons send through those pins*, not the pinout itself.\n\n- **Cruise control buttons (front of wheel)** \u2014 analog resistive ladder. Each button drops a different resistance to ground across the S/C Signal lines; the PCM reads resistance and identifies the button. Works as-is on a 2009 JK provided it already has factory cruise.\n- **Horn** \u2014 dedicated wires. Always works after the swap with a matching 2011+ airbag.\n- **Airbag** \u2014 dedicated wires. Works only with a matching 2011+ airbag \u2014 the 2007\u20132010 airbag mounting style is incompatible with 2011+ wheels (clip vs bolt).\n- **Audio/radio buttons (back of wheel)** \u2014 LIN bus digital. The 2011+ wheel has a switch module inside it that encodes presses as digital LIN messages on pin 2. The signal goes to the instrument cluster, which decodes the LIN and rebroadcasts on the CAN-IHS bus (message ID `3A3`) for the factory radio.\n\n**The compatibility wall.** The 2009 instrument cluster has no firmware for reading or rebroadcasting the 2011+ wheel's LIN audio messages. The wire is physically present but nothing in the 2009 vehicle is listening. Same applies to left-hand EVIC menu buttons. This is the entire reason the audio buttons appear dead after a naive swap.\n\n**CAN message reference (3A3, byte 0):** `01` next mode, `02` volume up, `04` volume down, `08` channel/seek up, `10` channel/seek down, `20` next preset, `40` previous preset. A 2009 cluster never generates these from a 2011+ wheel's LIN inputs.\n\n**Three paths for audio button function:**\n\n1. **Cruise only (cheapest).** Install the wheel, airbag, and 2011+ clockspring. Cruise, horn, airbag all work. Audio buttons are dead. No interface module needed.\n2. **Aftermarket head unit + interface module (most common).** Replace the 2009 factory radio with an aftermarket head unit and add an interface module (iDatalink Maestro RR2 or PAC SWI-CP5) that translates the LIN signals into a format the new radio understands.\n3. **DIY ESP32 bridge.** Documented on JK-Forum: ESP32 microcontroller with LIN and CAN transceivers reads LIN button presses and outputs analog resistance values (resistive ladder) to a head unit's KEY1/KEY2 inputs. Functional but requires soldering and Arduino programming.\n\n**Head unit options (for path 2):**\n\n- *Alpine i509-WRA-JK* (~$1,200) \u2014 Jeep-specific 9\" floating screen, wireless CarPlay/Android Auto, iDatalink module included. Easiest install.\n- *Stinger HEIGH10 RB10JK11B* (~$900\u2013$1,100) \u2014 10\" floating touchscreen, JK-specific bezel kit, CarPlay/AA, retains and remaps SWC.\n- *Kenwood/Pioneer/JVC + iDatalink Maestro RR2* (~$500\u2013$700) \u2014 Most flexible. Kenwood DMX7706S or current equivalent; Maestro pipes vehicle data (coolant temp, voltage, TPMS) to the head unit screen. Crutchfield auto-includes the correct dash kit, antenna adapter, and Maestro harness when you enter the vehicle.\n- *Scosche LUSWC* (~$60) \u2014 Plug-and-play SWC interface, remappable via PC. Works with most aftermarket head units. Does **not** make buttons work with the factory 2009 radio.\n\n**Critical Maestro warning for this swap.** The Maestro RR2 firmware for a stock 2009 JK (`ADS-RR(SR)-CHR01`) listens to the factory cluster's CAN-IHS broadcasts. Since the 2009 cluster doesn't broadcast audio button messages from a 2012 wheel, the Maestro on default firmware won't see them. In order of effort: (1) flash the Maestro as a 2012 JK with the audio button package \u2014 some installers report this works because Maestro can listen for LIN directly, others don't; (2) use PAC SWI-CP5 instead \u2014 it taps the LIN bus wire directly at the clockspring, bypassing the cluster entirely (most reliable for a non-stock build like this); (3) swap the 2011+ instrument cluster along with the wheel \u2014 expensive, involves PCM/security reflashing via Mopar TechAuthority.\n\n**Recommended approach:** Try Maestro flashed as a 2012. If audio buttons don't register after a Maestro reset and re-flash, pivot to PAC SWI-CP5 (~$70).\n\n**Maestro LED diagnostics:** Solid green = paired and reading the bus. Flashing red once = no firmware, re-flash via Weblink. Flashing red rapidly on button press = sees the press but can't map it, enable SWC in head unit settings.\n\n**Maestro reset procedure:** Key OFF. Unplug all connectors. Hold the programming button while reconnecting. LED flashes red rapidly. Release button. Solid green for 2 seconds = reset complete.\n\n**What the Maestro retains beyond button function:** retained accessory power (radio plays ~10 min after key off until door opens), reverse trigger (back-up camera switches on automatically), parking brake signal (video plays only when stopped), vehicle speed pulse for nav, and engine data on the head unit screen if it supports it.\n\n**Budget estimate (Path B \u2014 Maestro path):** Used 2011+ wheel $150\u2013$300, used airbag $100\u2013$200, Crown 5156106AD clockspring $80\u2013$120, aftermarket head unit $300\u2013$700, Maestro RR2 + harness $150\u2013$250, PAC SWI-CP5 backup $70. Total: **$850\u2013$1,640**.","steps":["Center the wheels (drive straight, then park).","Disconnect negative battery cable. Wait 15 minutes for the airbag capacitor to discharge.","Pry the three airbag retaining clips at 10, 2, and 6 o'clock with a dental pick or small flathead. Do 10 and 2 first, then 6.","Lift the airbag out, unplug the airbag connectors and the horn wire.","Unplug the cruise control connector and the clockspring connector at 12 o'clock.","Remove the 13mm steering wheel bolt and pull the wheel straight off the splines.","Remove the three T20 screws holding the old clockspring.","Transfer the wiper stalk and turn signal stalk to the new 2011+ clockspring (Mopar 68078303AC or Crown 5156106AD).","Install the new clockspring with the three T20 screws. Leave the yellow protector tab in place.","Center the new clockspring: rotate fully one direction counting turns until resistance, rotate back the same number of turns, then back half that count \u2014 that's center. Don't move it again until the wheel is on.","Slide the 2012 wheel onto the splines (keyed, one orientation only). Torque the 13mm bolt to 40 ft-lb. Pull the yellow tab out of the clockspring.","Plug in the cruise control connector (cruise + horn live here). Plug in the new airbag connectors. Plug in the audio button connector at 12 o'clock (the LIN bus line \u2014 needed for Maestro later). Snap the new airbag in until all three clips click.","Pull the factory radio (four screws behind the bezel). Unplug the 22-pin connector from the back.","Plug the HRN-AV-CH1 t-harness inline: vehicle 22-pin \u2192 t-harness \u2192 aftermarket radio harness. Connect Maestro module to the t-harness. Plug Maestro data cable into the head unit's data/remote port. Plug Maestro 3.5mm audio cable into head unit's aux input (Pioneer exception: rear AUX, not W/R). Leave radio loose in the dash for now.","Connect Maestro to PC via mini-USB. Log into idatalinkmaestro.com. Run Weblink: select **2012 Jeep Wrangler** with audio button package and your specific head unit. Let it flash. Disconnect from PC, reinstall in dash.","Reconnect negative battery cable. Turn key to ON (don't start). Maestro LED should go solid green. Verify airbag light comes on at key-on, then goes off after start. In head unit settings, turn **Steering Wheel Control: ON**. Test each button: cruise on/off, set, resume, cancel, volume up/down, track skip, source.","Slide radio into dash and screw down. Reattach radio bezel. Take a short drive to verify cruise engages and holds."],"pros":["Wheel itself is a direct mechanical fit \u2014 same splines, same 6-pin clockspring connector","Cruise control, horn, and airbag transfer cleanly with the matching 2011+ airbag and clockspring","Modernizes 2007\u20132010 JK interior dramatically (leather, redesigned button layout)","With Maestro or PAC interface + aftermarket head unit, you also gain CarPlay/Android Auto, backup camera, and vehicle telemetry on the dash","Budget path (cruise + horn + airbag only, no audio button function) is under $400"],"cons":["2007\u20132010 airbag does NOT mount on 2011+ wheel (clip vs bolt) \u2014 must source a 2011+ airbag, which is expensive used and harder to find than a wheel","Audio buttons will not work with the factory 2009 radio under any configuration \u2014 the cluster firmware is the limit","Maestro on default 2009 firmware won't see audio button presses; needs a 2012 re-flash or pivot to PAC SWI-CP5","2011+ instrument cluster swap (the \"thorough\" fix) requires PCM/security reflashing through Mopar TechAuthority or a locksmith","Airbag work \u2014 disconnect battery, wait for capacitor discharge, handle the SRS module carefully. A mistake here can deploy the airbag in your face."]},{"id":"jk-battery-cables-jk","title":"Battery Cable Inspection and Replacement","vehicle":"jk","category":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":25,"high":250},"tools_required":["10mm socket","13mm socket","wire brush","battery terminal cleaner","dielectric grease","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","electrical safety"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2007-2011","2012-2018"],"tags":["electrical","starting","charging","no-start"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar OEM positive battery cable","part_number":"68252091AB","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":95},{"name":"Mopar OEM negative battery cable","part_number":"68252090AB","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":65},{"name":"Custom Battery Cables JK Pentastar military kit","part_number":"7240M","vendor":"Custom Battery Cables","price_estimate_usd":230},{"name":"NOCO NCP2 battery terminal cleaner spray","part_number":"NCP2","vendor":"NOCO","price_estimate_usd":8},{"name":"Permatex dielectric grease (3 oz)","part_number":"22058","vendor":"Permatex","price_estimate_usd":7}],"citations":[{"title":"Custom Battery Cables \u2014 JK Pentastar Military Kit #7240M","url":"https://custombatterycables.com/jeep-wrangler/"},{"title":"Quadratec \u2014 How to Replace Your Jeep Wrangler Battery and Upgrade Terminals","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/c/howto/how-to-replace-your-jeep-wrangler-battery"},{"title":"Wrangler Forum \u2014 Battery Terminal Corrosion","url":"https://www.wranglerforum.com/threads/battery-terminal-corrosion.2457921/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Corroded or undersized battery cables are the most common cause of slow cranking, intermittent no-starts, and phantom electrical gremlins on the JK \u2014 and the fix is under an hour with hand tools.","description":"The JK's factory battery cables are adequate when new but age poorly. The negative cable in particular runs to a body ground that sees moisture and road salt; the positive cable's stamped lug at the starter solenoid corrodes under heat. By 100,000 miles, voltage drop across one or both cables can climb high enough to confuse the PCM and produce slow starts, dim headlights at idle, and stored codes that point everywhere except the actual cable.\n\nThe first job is inspection. Pop the hood, pull the rubber boots off both terminals, and look for white or green powder under the insulation at the lug-to-cable crimp. That powder is corrosion that wicked up the strands; once it's there, no amount of wire-brushing the terminal will restore connection \u2014 the cable is done. While you're in there, follow the negative cable to its body ground and pull the bolt; if the bolt or ground pad is rusty, clean both with a wire brush and reinstall with dielectric grease.\n\nIf you're replacing, you have three paths. Mopar OEM cables are direct fits and run about $95 positive / $65 negative. They will work and look stock. The second path is a military-style terminal upgrade: cut the old terminal off your existing cable, crimp on a new bolt-on military terminal, and call it done \u2014 under $25 in parts. The third path is a full custom cable upgrade kit like the Custom Battery Cables #7240M. Heavier gauge wire, military terminals, fused alternator lead, and proper chassis grounds. About $230, and worth it on a JK with a winch, big stereo, or auxiliary lights.\n\nDisconnect order matters. Pull negative first, always. Reverse on reinstall \u2014 positive on first, negative last. This prevents shorting a wrench from positive to chassis ground. Torque the terminal bolts to 70 in-lb (Mopar spec). Hand-tight plus a snug turn is fine if you don't have an in-lb wrench \u2014 overtightening crushes the soft lead post.\n\nAfter install, smear dielectric grease over the terminals and re-cover the boots. Crank the engine. If the start is noticeably faster than before, the cables were the problem.","steps":["Disconnect the negative battery cable first (10mm). Move it well clear of the post.","Disconnect the positive cable (10mm).","Trace each cable to its other end \u2014 starter solenoid for positive, body ground for negative. Inspect for corrosion, chafe, or burnt insulation.","If replacing the positive cable, the lug at the starter is 13mm. Access is from below on most JKs.","If replacing the negative cable, pull the body ground bolt and clean the pad to bare metal.","Install new cables in reverse order. Apply dielectric grease at every connection.","Torque battery terminal bolts to 70 in-lb. Reinstall positive first, then negative.","Start the engine and verify crank speed and voltage at idle (target: 13.8\u201314.4V)."],"pros":["Fixes slow cranking, no-starts, and voltage-drop electrical gremlins","Cheap if you re-terminate; modest cost if you replace whole cables","No special tools beyond basic hand tools","Often resolves codes the cables themselves never throw"],"cons":["Easy to miss if you only inspect the visible terminal \u2014 corrosion hides under insulation","OEM cable lugs are crimped, not soldered \u2014 long-term reliability is mediocre on a daily-driven JK","Aftermarket military terminal kits require crimping or soldering \u2014 first-timers need to practice"]},{"id":"jk-coolant-flush-jk","title":"Coolant Flush \u2014 3.8L and 3.6L Pentastar","vehicle":"jk","category":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1.5,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":40,"high":90},"tools_required":["drain pan","5/8\" hose","funnel","channel lock pliers","ratchet set"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","fluid handling"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2007-2011","2012-2018"],"tags":["cooling","preventative"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar HOAT (pink) 50/50 premix \u2014 2007-2012 only","part_number":"68163848AB","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":22},{"name":"Mopar OAT (purple/MS-12106) 50/50 premix \u2014 2013+","part_number":"68163850AB","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":24},{"name":"Distilled water (1 gallon, for top-off and flush rinse)","vendor":"any grocery","price_estimate_usd":2}],"citations":[{"title":"JK-Forum coolant change writeup","url":"https://www.jk-forum.com/how-tos/a/jeep-wrangler-jk-how-to-change-coolant-407878"},{"title":"Simi Valley CDJR \u2014 2017 JK coolant capacity","url":"https://www.simivalleychryslerdodgejeepram.com/blogs/5737/the-coolant-capacity-of-the-2017-jeep-wrangler-jk"},{"title":"WranglerForum cooling system flush thread","url":"https://www.wranglerforum.com/threads/flushing-your-jks-cooling-system.2356387/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Drain the radiator, flush with distilled water until clear, then refill with the correct Mopar coolant for your year \u2014 HOAT for 2007\u20132012, OAT for 2013+. The two chemistries are not interchangeable.","description":"The JK cooling system holds about 11.4 quarts when fully dry, but a drain-and-fill through the radiator petcock only swaps roughly 8 quarts. That is enough to refresh the chemistry on a healthy system every 5 years or 60,000 miles. If you are chasing a contamination problem \u2014 sludge, rust color, oil sheen \u2014 you need a full flush with distilled water, not a simple drain.\n\nThe single most important rule is matching coolant type to model year. 2007 through 2012 JKs were filled with HOAT (Hybrid Organic Additive Technology \u2014 Mopar's pink coolant). 2013 through 2018 JKs switched to OAT (Mopar MS-12106, purple). Chrysler says explicitly that mixing the two causes accelerated corrosion and seal failure. If you are not sure what is in the system, flush it completely with distilled water before adding new fluid.\n\nA few JK-specific notes that catch people. The petcock on the lower radiator passenger side is plastic and brittle \u2014 turn it gently, replace it if it weeps after reinstall. The pressure cap sits on the coolant bottle, not the radiator. The 3.6L Pentastar (2012+) is fussy about air pockets \u2014 leave the cap off, run the engine to operating temperature with the heater on full hot, and top off the bottle as the level drops. Skipping the bleed step is how people end up with a hot gauge two days later.\n\nFor a full flush: drain at the petcock, then run a garden hose through the upper radiator hose port with the engine off and the lower hose disconnected. Flush until the water runs clear. Reconnect, refill with 50/50 premix to the MAX line on the bottle, run to temperature with heater on high, and top off again after cooldown.","steps":["Park on level ground, engine cold. Remove the pressure cap from the coolant bottle.","Place a drain pan under the radiator. Open the lower petcock (passenger side, plastic \u2014 turn gently).","Drain fully \u2014 about 8 quarts will come out. Close the petcock.","For a full flush only: disconnect the lower radiator hose, run distilled water through the upper hose port until it runs clear out the bottom.","Refill the coolant bottle with the correct Mopar premix to the MAX line.","Start the engine with the cap off, heater on full hot. Run to operating temperature (about 15 minutes).","Top off the bottle as the level drops while air burps out.","Install the cap, drive for a heat cycle, let cool, recheck the level the next morning."],"pros":["Maintenance you can do in your driveway with $50 of fluid","Removes the silicate sludge that clogs heater cores and radiators","Avoids the $200+ shop charge for the same procedure","Catches petcock and hose problems before they strand you"],"cons":["A drain-and-fill only exchanges 70% of the fluid","Plastic petcock is fragile \u2014 keep a spare on hand for older Jeeps","Mixing HOAT and OAT requires a full system flush to undo","Pentastar air pockets can hide for a day or two if you skip the bleed"]},{"id":"jk-differential-fluid","title":"Front and Rear Differential Fluid Change","vehicle":"jk","category":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":40,"high":90},"tools_required":["3/8 ratchet","13mm socket or 3/8 square drive","fluid pump","drain pan","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","fluid handling"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2007-2018"],"tags":["axles","fluids","dana-30","dana-44"],"parts":[{"name":"Valvoline SynPower 75W-90 GL-5 (quart)","vendor":"AutoZone/RockAuto","price_estimate_usd":13},{"name":"Mopar 75W-140 Synthetic Gear Oil","part_number":"68218057AB","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"Mopar Limited Slip Additive (Rubicon Trac-Lok)","part_number":"04318060AC","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":12},{"name":"Differential cover gasket (RTV alternative)","vendor":"Permatex Ultra Black","price_estimate_usd":9}],"citations":[{"title":"Wrangler JK differential fluid write-up","url":"https://www.wayalife.com/threads/maintenance-jeep-jk-wrangler-differential-fluid-change-write-up.7836/"},{"title":"Jeep Wrangler differential fluid capacity chart \u2014 Motobilt","url":"https://motobilt.com/blogs/tech/jeep-wrangler-differential-fluid-capacity-chart"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Both differentials on a JK should see fresh gear oil every 30,000 miles on the street and after every water crossing deep enough to submerge the axle tubes.","description":"The JK's front axle is a Dana 30 on Sport/Sahara models and a Dana 44 on Rubicons. The rear is a Dana 44 across the board (Dana 35 on very early Sport trims is rare). Fluid changes are the cheapest insurance you can buy for an open or locked diff \u2014 and they're nearly identical front to rear. The factory schedule says \"inspect at 30k,\" but on a wheeled rig that sees mud, water, or dust, plan to drain and refill at 30k regardless.\n\nCapacities to know: front Dana 30 holds about 1.05 quarts, front Dana 44 holds 1.35 quarts, rear Dana 44 holds 2.375 quarts. Use 75W-90 GL-5 synthetic for most use cases. Step up to 75W-140 if you tow heavy, run 35\"+ tires, or live somewhere hot like Phoenix. Rubicons with factory Trac-Lok rear diffs require limited-slip additive \u2014 Mopar 04318060AC, one bottle per fill. Electronic lockers on the Rubicon do not need additive.\n\nBoth diffs have a fill plug at the 9\u201310 o'clock position on the cover and a drain plug at the bottom. Open the fill plug first \u2014 if it won't budge with the diff full, you're stuck with no way to refill. Drain into a pan, inspect the magnet on the drain plug or inside the cover. A light gray paste is normal. Glittery metal flakes or chunks mean stop and investigate before you put fresh oil back in. Some owners pull the cover entirely on the first service to clean the housing and inspect the ring and pinion \u2014 worth doing once if you bought the rig used.\n\nReinstall the drain plug, then pump fresh oil through the fill hole until it dribbles out. That's the level \u2014 don't try to \"top it up\" past that, the breather will push the excess out and make a mess. Torque both plugs to 25 ft-lbs. If you ran the cover off, scrape both surfaces clean, lay a 3/16\" bead of Ultra Black RTV, let it skin for 10 minutes, then bolt the cover on at 30 ft-lbs in a crisscross pattern. Let it cure four hours before refilling.","steps":["Drive the rig 10 minutes to warm the oil \u2014 it drains faster and carries more contamination out.","Park level, set the parking brake, chock the front tires.","Open the fill plug first (3/8 square drive on most JKs). If it spins, you're cleared to drain.","Place a drain pan under the diff and open the drain plug.","Inspect the magnet and used oil. Note any abnormal debris.","Reinstall the drain plug at 25 ft-lbs.","Pump fresh 75W-90 (or 75W-140) into the fill hole until it overflows.","Add limited-slip additive first on Rubicon rear if applicable.","Reinstall the fill plug at 25 ft-lbs.","Repeat for the second axle. Dispose of used oil at any auto parts store."],"pros":["Cheap insurance against ring-and-pinion failure","Catches water intrusion before it eats bearings","1\u20132 hours total for both axles","No special tools beyond a fluid pump"],"cons":["Trac-Lok additive is a separate purchase you can't skip","Cover gasket service adds cure time","Used gear oil smells terrible and stains everything"]},{"id":"jk-oil-change-jk","title":"Oil Change \u2014 Pentastar 3.6L Full Procedure","vehicle":"jk","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"engine_oil","type":"task","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":45,"high":90},"tools_required":["13mm socket","oil filter cap socket (76mm, 14-flute)","drain pan (6+ quart)","torque wrench (in-lb capable)","floor jack and stands (optional)","shop rags"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2012-2018"],"tags":["oil-change","pentastar","3.6l","maintenance","filter"],"parts":[{"name":"5W-20 full synthetic oil (6 quarts)","part_number":"MS-12633","vendor":"Mopar / equivalent SN+ rated","price_estimate_usd":32},{"name":"Mopar oil filter (cartridge)","part_number":"68191349AC","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":9},{"name":"Drain plug crush washer","part_number":"6104787AA","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":1}],"citations":[{"title":"FCA 2012-2018 JK Wrangler service info \u2014 3.6L Pentastar oil capacity and spec","url":"https://www.moparrepairconnect.com"},{"title":"Pentastar 3.6L filter housing torque spec (25 Nm / 18 ft-lb)","url":"https://www.jk-forum.com"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Use 6 quarts of 5W-20 full synthetic and a 76mm cartridge filter. Drain plug torques to 20 ft-lb on a fresh crush washer; the plastic filter cap torques to 18 ft-lb (25 Nm). Total job runs 30\u201360 minutes.","description":"The 3.6L Pentastar that arrived in JKs for 2012 uses a top-mount cartridge filter \u2014 a plastic cap on top of the engine, not a spin-on canister underneath. This is the part most owners coming from the 3.8L V6 or a different platform get wrong on their first change.\n\n**Oil spec.** Mopar calls for 5W-20 meeting MS-6395. Any quality full synthetic with an API SN or SN+ rating works \u2014 there's no advantage to running OEM-branded oil. Capacity is 6 US quarts with filter change. Overfilling past the upper mark on the dipstick can cause the crankcase pressure to push oil into the intake through the PCV system, which causes long-term carbon issues on the intake valves (Pentastars are direct-injected and already prone to this).\n\n**Filter.** The cartridge filter sits in a plastic housing on top of the engine, just behind the throttle body. Use a 76mm 14-flute cap socket \u2014 the housing cracks if you use channel locks or a strap wrench because the plastic gets brittle after heat cycles. Mopar 68191349AC is the OEM filter and includes a new O-ring. Wam-Auto and FRAM CH11665 are functional equivalents.\n\n**Drain plug.** 13mm hex on the oil pan, passenger side. Torque is 20 ft-lb on a fresh crush washer \u2014 don't reuse the old washer; they're a dollar each and a leak means redoing the job.","steps":["Warm the engine for 2\u20133 minutes to thin the oil, then shut it off.","Remove the engine cover (pull straight up \u2014 friction fit).","Loosen the oil filter cap one turn to break the seal so oil drains into the housing before you remove the pan plug. This keeps the cap area cleaner.","Position drain pan under the oil pan plug. Remove the 13mm plug. Let drain 5+ minutes.","Remove the filter cap completely, lift out the old cartridge. Wipe the housing with a clean rag.","Pull the old O-ring off the cap. Install the new O-ring (lubricate with fresh oil). Snap the new cartridge into the cap.","Reinstall the cap. Torque to 25 Nm (18 ft-lb). Do not overtighten \u2014 the plastic threads strip.","Install drain plug with new crush washer. Torque to 20 ft-lb.","Pour in 6 quarts of 5W-20 synthetic through the cap. Start the engine; verify oil pressure light goes out within 3 seconds.","Shut off, wait 5 minutes, check level on dipstick. Top off if needed but do not overfill."],"pros":["Top-mount filter \u2014 no crawling under the truck","Standard 13mm drain plug","Common parts at any auto parts store","30-60 minute job once you've done it"],"cons":["Plastic filter housing cracks easily \u2014 must use cap socket","Stripped cap threads require housing replacement (~$80 part)","Filter cap location means oil drips down the engine if you don't loosen it before pulling the drain plug"]},{"id":"jk-serpentine-belt","title":"Serpentine Belt Replacement \u2014 3.6L","vehicle":"jk","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"belts_hoses","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":35,"high":70},"tools_required":["3/8 ratchet","1/2 breaker bar","15mm socket","serpentine belt tool or long 3/8 extension"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2012-2018"],"tags":["belts","engine","maintenance"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar OEM serpentine belt","part_number":"5281454AA","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":55},{"name":"Gates Micro-V K060867","part_number":"K060867","vendor":"Gates","price_estimate_usd":28},{"name":"Dayco serpentine belt","part_number":"5060870","vendor":"Dayco","price_estimate_usd":32},{"name":"OMIX belt tensioner (if replacing)","part_number":"17112.17","vendor":"OMIX-ADA / Crown","price_estimate_usd":45}],"citations":[{"title":"Replacing the Pentastar 3.6L Drive Belt","url":"http://www.wanderingtrail.com/Mechanical/2012JK/36LDriveBelt/36LDriveBelt.html"},{"title":"JK-Forum: How to Replace Serpentine Belt","url":"https://www.jk-forum.com/how-tos/a/jeep-wrangler-jk-how-to-replace-serpentine-belt-407279"},{"title":"Quadratec OMIX 17112.17 Tensioner","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/products/51217_0121_14.htm"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The 3.6L Pentastar serpentine belt is a 30-minute job with one tensioner pulley and a single belt \u2014 most owners do it in the driveway with a 3/8 ratchet and a long extension.","description":"The 2012\u20132018 JK with the 3.6L Pentastar runs a single six-rib serpentine belt about 87.5 inches long. Mopar's OEM belt (5281454AA) is the safe choice; Gates K060867 and Dayco equivalents are commonly used aftermarket replacements at roughly half the price. Inspect the belt for glazing, missing chunks, or rib cracking every 30k miles \u2014 Pentastar belts typically last 60k\u2013100k miles depending on climate. Phoenix heat shortens that window. If the belt squeals on cold start or after rain, the tensioner is more often the culprit than the belt itself.\n\nThe tensioner pulley on the Pentastar uses a hydraulic damper to control bounce under load. When it fails, you'll hear chirping or rattling at idle, or see the belt visibly oscillating. Replacement tensioners (OMIX 17112.17 or Mopar equivalent) run $45\u2013$75 and install with a single 15mm bolt torqued to roughly 23\u201324 ft-lbs. Replacing the tensioner at the same time as the belt is smart preventive work \u2014 both wear together and you've already got the belt off.\n\nRouting on the JK 3.6L runs the belt around the crankshaft, water pump, alternator, A/C compressor, idler, power steering pump, and tensioner. There's a routing diagram sticker under the hood near the radiator support. Photograph it before pulling the belt off \u2014 even with a diagram, getting the belt back on the alternator pulley with one hand while holding the tensioner with the other is the hardest part of the job.\n\nUse a 3/8 ratchet with a long extension on the square hole in the tensioner arm to rotate it counterclockwise and release belt tension. Slip the belt off the alternator pulley first (it has the most clearance), then walk it off the rest. Reverse the process to install the new belt, saving the alternator pulley for last. Verify routing matches the diagram before starting the engine.","steps":["Park on level ground, engine cold. Disconnect the negative battery terminal if removing the tensioner.","Photograph the belt routing diagram under the hood and the current belt path.","Place a 3/8 ratchet with long extension into the square drive on the tensioner arm.","Rotate the tensioner counterclockwise to release tension on the belt.","Slip the belt off the alternator pulley while holding the tensioner.","Slowly release the tensioner back to rest position.","Remove the belt fully and compare its length and rib count to the new belt.","Route the new belt around all pulleys except the alternator, matching the diagram.","Rotate the tensioner counterclockwise again and slip the belt over the alternator pulley.","Release the tensioner slowly to apply tension.","Verify the belt is fully seated in every pulley groove.","Start the engine and confirm no squeal or visible misalignment."],"pros":["30-minute job with basic tools","$30 belt vs. $300+ at a shop","Eliminates squeal and vibration when paired with tensioner replacement","Catches a failing tensioner before it strands you"],"cons":["Belt routing is tight \u2014 getting the last pulley back on is the hard part","Tensioner bolt is hidden behind accessories on some trims","Cheap aftermarket belts can stretch and squeal within 10k miles"]},{"id":"jk-spark-plugs-jk","title":"Spark Plug Replacement \u2014 3.8L and 3.6L","vehicle":"jk","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"ignition","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2.5},"cost_usd":{"low":35,"high":90},"tools_required":["10mm socket","5/8\" spark plug socket (3.8L) or 14mm thin-wall plug socket (3.6L)","3/8\" ratchet and 6\" extension","torque wrench","anti-seize (use sparingly)","dielectric grease"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","torque awareness"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2007-2018"],"tags":["spark-plugs","ignition","3.8l","pentastar","tune-up"],"parts":[{"name":"NGK ZFR5LP-13G platinum (3.8L, qty 6)","part_number":"5306","vendor":"NGK","price_estimate_usd":35},{"name":"NGK PLZKAR6A-11 iridium (3.6L, qty 6)","part_number":"1480","vendor":"NGK","price_estimate_usd":75},{"name":"Champion RE10PMC5 OEM Pentastar plug","part_number":"RE10PMC5","vendor":"Champion","price_estimate_usd":60}],"citations":[{"title":"FCA Mopar 3.6L Pentastar service interval \u2014 100,000 mi plug change","url":"https://www.moparrepairconnect.com"},{"title":"3.8L V6 spark plug spec NGK ZFR5LP-13G","url":"https://www.ngksparkplugs.com"},{"title":"JK-Forum: 3.6L coil-on-plug bolt torque 60 in-lb","url":"https://www.jk-forum.com"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The 3.8L uses two plugs per cylinder (12 total) and the 3.6L uses one per cylinder (6 total). Both are coil-on-plug. Gap and torque matter: 3.8L at 0.043\", 13 ft-lb; 3.6L at 0.043\", 13 ft-lb. Change interval is 30K (3.8L copper) or 100K (Pentastar iridium).","description":"The 3.8L V6 (2007\u20132011) and the 3.6L Pentastar (2012\u20132018) take very different approaches. Both are coil-on-plug systems, but the 3.8L runs a dual-plug-per-cylinder setup for emissions \u2014 two plugs, two coil packs on each side of each cylinder, twelve plugs total. The Pentastar runs one plug per cylinder with a single coil-on-plug at each, six total.\n\n**3.8L plug choice.** Mopar spec is NGK ZFR5LP-13G platinum, gapped at 0.043\". You can run copper plugs (NGK ZFR5N) for cheaper if you don't mind changing them every 30K miles. The platinum plugs go 60K. Iridium upgrades are pointless on this engine \u2014 the 3.8L doesn't reward fancier plugs and they cost 3x more.\n\n**3.6L plug choice.** Mopar spec is the Champion RE10PMC5 (also sold as NGK PLZKAR6A-11), iridium, gapped at 0.043\". Service interval is 100K miles. Do not regap iridium plugs \u2014 the precious metal tip is thin and chips. They come pre-gapped from the factory; check with a wire gauge but don't bend.\n\n**Access.** 3.8L driver-side plugs require pulling the intake plenum on the rear three. Plan for 2+ hours if it's your first time. 3.6L is dramatically better \u2014 pull the engine cover, disconnect 6 coil connectors, remove 6 coil bolts, and the plugs are right there.\n\n**Torque is critical.** Pentastar uses aluminum heads. Overtightening strips threads, undertightening lets the plug back out under combustion pressure. Use a torque wrench. 13 ft-lb on both engines.","steps":["Engine cold. Disconnect negative battery cable.","**3.6L:** Pull the engine cover (friction fit). Disconnect each coil connector. Remove the 10mm coil hold-down bolt. Pull each coil straight up.","**3.8L:** Same idea but you have a coil on each side of each cylinder. Driver-side rear three require intake plenum removal \u2014 pull the 4 intake bolts and lift it aside, leaving fuel lines connected if possible.","Use compressed air to blow debris out of the plug well before pulling the plug \u2014 dirt falling into the cylinder is a real risk.","Use a magnetic or rubber-insert plug socket to back out the old plug. Check the porcelain for cracking and the electrode for excessive wear (gap should still be within 0.005\" of spec).","Verify gap on new plugs with a wire gauge (3.6L: 0.043\"; 3.8L: 0.043\"). Do not regap iridium.","Skip anti-seize on factory-coated plugs (most modern plugs come coated). If you must use it, a pinhead-sized dab on the threads only \u2014 never on the tip or seat.","Thread plug by hand until snug, then torque to 13 ft-lb.","Apply dielectric grease to the inside of the coil boot. Install coil. Torque the 10mm bolt to 60 in-lb (5 ft-lb).","Reconnect coil connectors. Reinstall engine cover. Reconnect battery. Start, listen for misfire."],"pros":["3.6L: 100K interval and 1-hour job","Coil-on-plug = no spark plug wires to fail","Common NGK / Champion / Denso parts","No misfire codes if torqued correctly"],"cons":["3.8L driver-side requires intake plenum removal","12 plugs total on 3.8L = double the work","Aluminum heads punish overtorquing","Wrong-gap iridium plugs cause misfires under load"]},{"id":"jk-transfer-case-fluid-jk","title":"Transfer Case Fluid Change \u2014 NV241 / NV271","vehicle":"jk","category":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":20,"high":40},"tools_required":["3/8 ratchet","13mm socket","fluid pump","drain pan","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2007-2018"],"tags":["fluids","drivetrain","preventive"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar ATF+4 (1 quart)","part_number":"68218057AC","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":10},{"name":"Valvoline MaxLife ATF (replacement for ATF+4)","vendor":"Valvoline","price_estimate_usd":8}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep JK Wrangler NV-241 / NV-241OR Transfer Case Service","url":"https://project-jk.com/jeep-jk-write-ups/jeep-jk-wrangler-nv-241-nv-241or-transfer-case-service"},{"title":"WAYALIFE NV-241 Transfer Case Service write-up","url":"https://www.wayalife.com/threads/maintenance-jeep-jk-wrangler-nv-241-nv-241or-transfer-case-service.5383/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The JK's NV241 (Sport/Sahara) and NV241OR (Rubicon) transfer cases need a fresh fill of ATF+4 every 30,000 miles \u2014 under an hour of work that saves a $2,000 t-case.","description":"Both the NV241 and the heavier-duty NV241OR in your JK use ATF+4 \u2014 not gear oil. Capacity is right around 2 quarts; you'll know the case is full when fluid starts trickling back out the fill plug. Skip this service and the chain rides in increasingly contaminated fluid until the bearings or chain themselves start to wear. Replacement t-cases run $1,500 to $2,500. A $20 fluid change is not a hard decision.\n\nPull the JK up on level ground and crack the fill plug first. This is the rule that has saved more drivetrains than any other: if the fill plug won't loosen, you don't want to discover that after the drain plug is empty on the floor. Both plugs are 3/8\" square drive \u2014 no socket needed, your ratchet fits directly. The fill plug is the upper one on the rear face of the case; the drain is below it.\n\nWith the drain pan ready, pull the drain plug and let it run for about ten minutes. Old fluid will be dark brown or black; it should come out red. Inspect the magnetic drain plug \u2014 a light fuzz of metal is normal, chunks or shavings are not. Clean the plug, install it, torque to 20\u201325 ft-lb (don't overdo it; the case is aluminum).\n\nPump new ATF+4 into the fill hole with a manual fluid pump. Most people use about 2 quarts before fluid starts to weep back out. Buy 2 quarts to be safe. Stop pumping the moment fluid trickles out, let it settle, then install the fill plug at 20\u201325 ft-lb. Wipe everything down and check for leaks after your first drive.","steps":["Park on level ground and let the case cool to warm (not hot).","Crack the fill plug loose first to verify it will open. If it won't, stop and address that before draining.","Position drain pan under the t-case drain plug.","Remove drain plug and let fluid drain fully (about 10 minutes).","Inspect the magnetic drain plug. Light fuzz is normal; chunks mean trouble.","Clean drain plug threads and install. Torque to 20\u201325 ft-lb.","Pump fresh ATF+4 into the fill hole until fluid trickles back out (about 2 quarts).","Install the fill plug and torque to 20\u201325 ft-lb.","Start the engine, run through 4H and 4L to circulate fluid, then re-check fill level.","Check for leaks after a short drive."],"pros":["30-minute job saves a $2,000 transfer case","No special tools \u2014 plugs use a 3/8\" ratchet directly","Fluid is cheap and widely available"],"cons":["Easy to forget \u2014 most owners don't service the t-case until it's noisy","Aluminum threads strip if you over-torque","Fluid disposal requires a recycling drop-off"]},{"id":"jk-alignment-jk","title":"JK Alignment \u2014 Caster, Camber, and Toe After a Lift","vehicle":"jk","category":"steering","type":"task","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":100,"high":250},"tools_required":["alignment rack or string box","tape measure","metric wrench set","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["measuring angles","tie rod adjustment"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2007-2018"],"tags":["death-wobble","lift","geometry-correction"],"parts":[{"name":"Geometry correction brackets (caster fix)","vendor":"JKS / Synergy / Teraflex","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"Adjustable front track bar","vendor":"Synergy / Currie / Rock Krawler","price_estimate_usd":250}],"citations":[{"title":"Wrangler JK Wheel Alignment Specifications","url":"https://www.extremeterrain.com/jk-wheel-alignment-specifications-tech-guide.html"},{"title":"Jeep Wrangler JK Alignment guide","url":"https://www.jeepfan.com/tech/jeep-wrangler-jk-alignment/"},{"title":"Alignment specs for 2.5in lift discussion","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/threads/specs-for-alignment-with-2-5in-lift.1354301/"}],"alternatives":["jk-death-wobble-deep","jk-track-bar-jk"],"summary":"After any lift over 1.5 inches, your JK needs a fresh alignment with caster pulled back into the 4.5\u20135.0\u00b0 range and toe set to 1/16\"\u20131/8\" toe-in; ignore this and you get wandering, tire chop, and a faster path to death wobble.","description":"Stock JK caster is around 4.2\u00b0 at ride height. When you lift the front, the axle rotates down and forward, which drops caster. A 2.5\" lift typically loses 1.5\u20132\u00b0 of caster \u2014 leaving you near 2.5\u00b0, which feels vague on-center and lets the steering hunt. The fix is geometry correction: either adjustable lower control arms (push the axle back, tilt the pinion up) or cam-bolt / geometry brackets that re-rotate the axle at the LCA mount. Brackets are cheaper (about $90) and bolt-in. Adjustable LCAs cost more but give you tuning room and don't pinch shock travel.\n\nCamber on the JK is not adjustable from the factory \u2014 there are no slots in the upper or lower mounts. If a stock 2.5\" lift puts camber out of spec, you have a bent knuckle or worn ball joints, not an alignment problem. Confirm camber is between -0.5\u00b0 and +0.5\u00b0 before chasing other angles.\n\nToe is the one angle every owner can set in the driveway. Loosen the two clamps on the adjuster sleeve at the tie rod, rotate the sleeve, and re-measure. Target total toe-in of 1/16\" to 1/8\" (about 0.05\u00b0 to 0.10\u00b0 per side). Too much toe-in eats the inside of the front tires; toe-out makes the truck dart over expansion joints.\n\nCaster pulls 4.5\u20135.0\u00b0 after correction is the sweet spot for a 2.5\u20133.5\" lift on 35s. Going higher than 5.5\u00b0 can introduce driveline vibration if the front driveshaft angle is also steep \u2014 caster and pinion angle are linked because they rotate the same axle. If you go to a long-arm kit or a 4.5\"+ short-arm with correction brackets, you can usually get caster back to 4.7\u20135.0\u00b0 without driveline buzz.","steps":["Park on level ground, tires straight, full fuel tank.","Measure caster with a digital angle gauge on the upper ball joint stud or knuckle face.","If caster is below 3.5\u00b0, install geometry brackets or lengthen lower control arms until caster reads 4.5\u20135.0\u00b0.","Center the steering wheel by adjusting the front track bar length so the axle sits centered under the body.","Set toe: roll vehicle forward six feet, measure across tire treads at the front and back of the tires. Target front measurement 1/16\"\u20131/8\" shorter than the rear.","Lock the tie rod clamps with bolts on the bottom side of the rod so they don't catch on debris.","Test drive on a flat highway. Check for pull, wander, and on-center feel."],"pros":["Eliminates wandering and tire chop","Reduces death wobble risk","Restores predictable steering feel","Often DIY with a string box and angle gauge"],"cons":["Caster correction parts add cost on lifts above 2\"","Camber issues require knuckle or ball joint work, not alignment","Final fine-tune still benefits from a rack alignment ($100\u2013150)"]},{"id":"jk-death-wobble-deep","title":"Death Wobble \u2014 Root Causes and Systematic Fix","vehicle":"jk","category":"steering","subcategory":"diagnostics","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":8},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":1500},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","pry bar","torque wrench","metric sockets","tape measure"],"skills_required":["front-end inspection","torque to spec","patience"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2007-2011","2012-2018"],"tags":["death-wobble","safety-critical","front-end","track-bar","tie-rod"],"parts":[{"name":"OEM front track bar (Mopar)","part_number":"52126115AF","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":220},{"name":"Synergy HD adjustable track bar","part_number":"8052","vendor":"Synergy Mfg","price_estimate_usd":379},{"name":"Currie Currectlync HD tie rod (JK)","part_number":"CE-9701JK","vendor":"RockJock 4x4","price_estimate_usd":549},{"name":"Dynatrac HD ball joints (pair)","part_number":"DA60-2X1052-B","vendor":"Dynatrac","price_estimate_usd":460}],"citations":[{"title":"Quadratec \u2014 Jeep Death Wobble Q&A: Causes, Symptoms, Fixes","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/c/reference/what-is-jeep-death-wobble-causes-symptoms-fix-prevention"},{"title":"Jeep JK Guide \u2014 JK Death Wobble: Causes, Diagnosis & Fixes","url":"https://www.jeepjkguide.com/articles/maintenance/jk-death-wobble-causes-fixes/"},{"title":"WranglerForum \u2014 Steering damper is a band-aid, not a cure","url":"https://www.wranglerforum.com/threads/if-steering-damper-is-just-a-band-aid-for-death-wobble-then-what-is-the-cure.2454296/"}],"alternatives":["jk-track-bar-jk","jk-tie-rods-jk"],"summary":"Death wobble on a JK is almost always loose or worn front-end components \u2014 most often the track bar \u2014 triggered by a bump or tire imbalance. The fix is to inspect every front-end joint, replace what is worn, and torque everything to spec. A steering stabilizer hides the symptom; it does not cure the cause.","description":"Death wobble is the violent side-to-side oscillation of the front axle that starts after hitting a bump at speed. It does not stop until you slow well below the trigger speed. On the JK platform (2007\u20132018), roughly seventy percent of cases trace back to the track bar \u2014 either worn frame-side or axle-side bushings, a loose mounting bolt, or wallowed-out bolt holes. The remainder split between worn tie rod and drag link ends, sloppy ball joints, loose control arm bushings, an out-of-balance or out-of-round tire, and worn wheel bearings.\n\n**Why JKs are prone to it.** Solid front axles steer with a linked drag-link-and-tie-rod setup that has no inherent self-damping. Add a lift, oversized tires, and any free play in the linkage, and a single bump can set the whole system resonating. The factory steering damper is a hydraulic shock absorber \u2014 it slows oscillation but cannot cancel it once worn parts let the axle move freely.\n\n**A stabilizer is not a fix.** New owners are often told \"throw a dual stabilizer on it.\" That masks the wobble at low amplitude and convinces you the truck is safe. It is not. Worn parts continue wearing, the wobble returns harder, and now your stabilizer is also worn out. Replace the cause, then keep the factory stabilizer if you want \u2014 it is sufficient on stock or mildly lifted JKs.\n\n**The systematic inspection.** Park on level concrete, key off, transmission in neutral, parking brake set. Have a helper rock the steering wheel side to side a few degrees while you watch each joint under the front of the truck. Every joint \u2014 track bar bolt ends, tie rod ends, drag link ends, ball joints, control arm bushings \u2014 should move as a single rigid system with the steering wheel. Any independent slop is a worn part. Grab the bottom and top of each front tire and rock it; in-out movement is a ball joint, side-to-side is a tie rod end. Check tire balance and runout last \u2014 a brand new tire with a thrown weight is a frequent trigger on a properly tight front end.\n\n**Torque matters.** Track bar bolts on a JK are 125 ft-lb at the axle and 55 ft-lb at the frame bracket. Tie rod jam nuts at the steering knuckle are 55 ft-lb. Ball joint nuts are 70 ft-lb upper and 80 ft-lb lower. Re-torque any time the front end has been apart or after a hard trail run.","steps":["Confirm symptoms \u2014 wobble at a specific speed, triggered by a bump, stops only when slowing well below trigger speed. Not road feedback or a single shake.","Inspect tires for imbalance, broken belts (visible bulge), or low pressure. Set pressure to door-jamb spec cold. If wobble stops, balance the tires.","Get the front wheels off the ground on jack stands. Grab each tire at 12 and 6 \u2014 any clunk is upper or lower ball joint. Grab at 9 and 3 \u2014 clunk is tie rod end or wheel bearing.","Have a helper turn the steering wheel slowly lock to lock while you watch every joint. The whole linkage should move together. Identify any joint with independent play.","Check the track bar bolts with a torque wrench at 125 ft-lb axle / 55 ft-lb frame. Pry on the track bar at the axle bracket with a pry bar \u2014 any movement is worn axle-side bushing or a wallowed bracket hole.","Check control arm bolts and bushings \u2014 visible cracking in rubber or hourglass-shaped voids means replacement.","Replace whatever is worn. Re-torque everything to spec.","Road test in a safe area, gradually approaching the speed where wobble occurred. Bring the truck up to highway speed only after a bump-strike test confirms it is stable."],"pros":["Restores safe highway driving","Catches worn parts before they fail completely","Cheap if it is only loose bolts ($0)","Doesn't require a shop visit"],"cons":["Diagnosis takes time and patience","Worst case (track bar + tie rod + ball joints) runs $1,000\u2013$1,500 in parts","A stabilizer alone will mask the issue and create a false sense of safety","Lifted trucks with oversized tires are more prone \u2014 geometry correction may also be needed"]},{"id":"jk-tie-rods-jk","title":"Tie Rod Replacement \u2014 Heavy Duty Upgrade Options","vehicle":"jk","category":"steering","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":350,"high":900},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","pickle fork or tie rod separator","ball joint press (optional)","metric sockets","torque wrench","tape measure"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","alignment understanding"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2007-2018"],"tags":["death-wobble","steering","upgrade"],"parts":[{"name":"Synergy HD Chromoly Tie Rod","part_number":"8002-15","vendor":"Synergy Manufacturing","price_estimate_usd":450},{"name":"Steer Smarts YETI XD Tie Rod","part_number":"78065001","vendor":"Steer Smarts","price_estimate_usd":800},{"name":"RockJock Currectlync Modular Extreme Duty Tie Rod","part_number":"JK-9703TR","vendor":"RockJock 4x4","price_estimate_usd":700}],"citations":[{"title":"Synergy 8002-15 Heavy Duty Chromoly Tie Rod","url":"https://www.extremeterrain.com/synergy-heavy-duty-chromoly-tie-rod-8002.html"},{"title":"Steer Smarts YETI XD Tie Rod Assembly","url":"https://steersmarts.com/products/yeti-jk-heavy-duty-tie-rod-assembly"},{"title":"RockJock JK-9703TR Currectlync Tie Rod","url":"https://www.rockjock4x4.com/JK-9703TR"}],"alternatives":["jk-track-bar-jk","jk-steering-stabilizer-jk"],"summary":"The stock JK tie rod is hollow stamped steel and bends on the first decent rock hit; a chromoly replacement from Synergy, Steer Smarts, or RockJock is the single most useful steering upgrade on the platform.","description":"The factory tie rod on a 2007\u20132018 Wrangler JK looks beefy but it's a thin-wall hollow tube with ball joints that wear quickly under big-tire loads. Once it's bent \u2014 even slightly \u2014 you'll chase alignment, get pull, and feel wandering at highway speed. A bent tie rod is also a common contributor to death wobble because the play it introduces lets the front axle oscillate freely.\n\nThree upgrades dominate the market. Synergy's 8002-15 is a 1.5\" 4130 chromoly tube with rebuildable metal-on-metal greasable ends, lands around $450, and is what most builders pick for daily-driven 33\"\u201337\" rigs. Steer Smarts' YETI XD (PN 78065001) runs about $800 and uses 30 mm reverse-pin ball joints \u2014 the largest in any JK steering linkage. RockJock's Currectlync (JK-9703TR) is the 2\u00bd-ton option with 1-5/8\" 4130 tube and 1-1/4\" forged rod ends; it's overkill for trail use and the right call for hard rockcrawling on 37\"+ tires.\n\nAvoid aluminum tie rods. They're light and look great but the threaded sleeve is the weak point \u2014 overtightened jam nuts crack the sleeve, and trail impacts can fracture the bar where steel would just bend. If you wheel hard, pick steel or chromoly.\n\nThe install is bolt-in. Measure your existing tie rod center-to-center before pulling it so you can set the new one to the same length and minimize toe disturbance. Always get a real alignment after the swap.","steps":["Park on level ground, set parking brake, chock the rear wheels. Loosen front lug nuts.","Jack the front axle and place jack stands under the frame rails. Pull both front wheels.","Measure and record the center-to-center length of the stock tie rod between the two TRE centers.","Remove the cotter pins and castle nuts from both tie rod ends at the steering knuckles. Torque spec for the knuckle nuts is 60 ft-lb on JK.","Separate each TRE from the knuckle with a tie rod separator (preferred) or pickle fork. Pull the tie rod assembly out.","Pre-assemble the new tie rod to match your recorded length, threading the TREs into the sleeve evenly on both sides.","Install the new assembly into the knuckles, install new castle nuts, torque to 60 ft-lb, and continue tightening just enough to align the cotter pin slots. Install fresh cotter pins.","Tighten the jam nuts or pinch clamps. Grease the TREs if they have zerks.","Reinstall wheels, torque lug nuts to 95 ft-lb, drop the truck, and drive carefully to a shop for an alignment."],"pros":["Eliminates a major death-wobble contributor","Survives rock contact without permanent damage","Greasable rebuildable ends on most kits","Direct bolt-in, no fabrication"],"cons":["Premium options run $700\u2013$900","Aluminum bargain options can fracture on impact","Alignment required after install"]},{"id":"jk-track-bar-jk","title":"Track Bar Replacement and Geometry","vehicle":"jk","category":"steering","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":180,"high":450},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","21mm socket","18mm socket","breaker bar","torque wrench","tape measure"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","axle centering measurement"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2007-2011","2012-2018"],"tags":["death-wobble","steering-geometry","axle-centering"],"parts":[{"name":"Synergy HD adjustable front track bar","part_number":"8550-01","vendor":"Synergy Manufacturing","price_estimate_usd":380},{"name":"Rough Country adjustable front track bar","part_number":"1180","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":190},{"name":"Currie CurrECTED Johnny Joint track bar","vendor":"Currie Enterprises","price_estimate_usd":320},{"name":"Mopar OEM replacement front track bar","part_number":"52126340AE","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":165}],"citations":[{"title":"JK Suspension Torque Specs \u2014 MetalCloak","url":"https://metalcloak.com/blog/jeep-wrangler-jk-suspension-torque-specs-the-complete-guide"},{"title":"Synergy JK Front Track Bar","url":"https://www.synergymfg.com/synergy-jeep-jk-front-track-bar.html"},{"title":"JK Wrangler Track Bar Torque Discussion","url":"https://www.wranglerforum.com/threads/torque-specs-for-track-bar-at-axle.575954/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The front track bar locates your axle side-to-side; replace it if you see steering wander, off-center steering wheel, or if you've added a lift that pushes the axle out from under the frame.","description":"The JK front track bar runs from the passenger-side frame rail down to the driver-side axle bracket. It's the single component that keeps the axle centered laterally as the suspension cycles. When the factory bar wears out \u2014 and the bushings always wear out around 80,000 miles \u2014 the axle starts drifting under load. You'll feel it as a wandering steering wheel, a clunk over bumps, or the dreaded death wobble. A worn track bar bushing is the most common death wobble trigger on the JK.\n\nAfter a 2.5\" or larger lift, the geometry changes. The track bar pulls the axle to the passenger side because the bar's effective length is wrong for the new ride height. An adjustable track bar lets you re-center the axle. Measure from the inside edge of each tire to the frame rail at the same point \u2014 they should match within 1/8\". If they don't, adjust the bar.\n\nBrand picks: Synergy 8550-01 ($380) is the gold standard with dual-durometer bushings and Teflon bearing \u2014 quiet on-road, durable on-trail. Currie's Johnny Joint version ($320) is rebuildable and articulates further but transmits more noise into the cabin. Rough Country's adjustable bar ($190) is the budget option and works fine for moderate lifts under 3.5\". For stock-height rigs replacing a worn factory bar, OEM Mopar 52126340AE ($165) is the most direct swap.\n\nInstall requires the Jeep at ride height for the final torque pass \u2014 bushings preload incorrectly if tightened with the suspension drooped. Pull the wheel for access, support the axle on a jack, remove the frame-side bolt first (125 ft-lbs), then the axle-side bolt (125 ft-lbs). Set the new bar to the same length as the old one as a starting point, install loosely, lower the Jeep to ride height, then torque both ends. Re-measure axle centering. Adjust if needed.","steps":["Park on level ground, set the parking brake, chock the rear wheels.","Loosen the front lug nuts, lift the front, set on jack stands under the frame, remove the front wheels.","Measure from each frame rail to the corresponding inner tire edge \u2014 record both numbers.","Support the front axle on a floor jack to remove tension from the track bar.","Remove the frame-side bolt (21mm head, 125 ft-lbs from factory).","Remove the axle-side bolt (18mm head, 125 ft-lbs from factory).","Compare new bar to old bar at equal eye-to-eye length.","Install new bar finger-tight, frame side first, then axle side.","Reinstall wheels, lower to ride height.","Torque both bolts to 125 ft-lbs at ride height.","Re-measure axle centering \u2014 adjust bar length if needed.","Drive 50 miles, re-check torque."],"pros":["Cures the most common JK death wobble trigger","Adjustable versions re-center axle after lift","HD construction resists bending on rocks","Bolt-in install, no welding"],"cons":["Factory torque-to-yield bolts should be replaced","Adjustable bars with Johnny Joints transmit road noise","Some adjustable bars require drilling axle mount to 9/16\"","Final torque must happen at ride height or bushings fail early"]},{"id":"jk-coil-spring-replacement","title":"Coil Spring Replacement","vehicle":"jk","category":"suspension","subcategory":"springs","type":"task","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":150,"high":600},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","spring compressor (rear only if compressed)","metric sockets","torque wrench","pry bar","ratchet straps"],"skills_required":["suspension disassembly","torque sequencing","alignment understanding"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2007-2018"],"tags":["springs","lift","ride-quality","sag"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar Heavy Duty Front Coil Spring (Rubicon/HD)","part_number":"52126318AC","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":95},{"name":"Mopar Front Coil Spring Right (Sport/Sahara)","part_number":"52126314AC","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":75},{"name":"Mopar Rear Coil Spring","part_number":"77070091","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":80},{"name":"Old Man Emu 2.5\" Heavy Duty Coil Set (JK)","vendor":"ARB / OME","price_estimate_usd":480},{"name":"Rough Country 2.5\" Coil Spring Set","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":210}],"citations":[{"title":"Mopar Heavy Duty Front Coil Spring 52126318AC","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/p/mopar/heavy-duty-front-coil-spring-wrangler-jk"},{"title":"Mopar Coil Spring 52126314AC","url":"https://parts.moparonlineparts.com/oem-parts/mopar-front-coil-spring-right-52126314ac"}],"alternatives":["jk-lift-kit-overview","jk-shock-replacement"],"summary":"Sagging or broken coil springs cause uneven ride height, poor bump absorption, and bottoming on trail \u2014 replace them in pairs (both fronts or both rears) with either Mopar OE or aftermarket lift springs sized to your build.","description":"Stock JK coil springs are progressive-rate units that settle over time, especially under bumpers, winches, and roof racks. After 100,000 miles, a stock spring can sag 0.5\"\u20131\" below its original free length. Mismatched ride height (one side lower than the other) usually points to one tired spring or a cracked coil \u2014 and it shows up at the wheel arch height before you feel it in the seat.\n\nYou have three replacement paths. First: Mopar OE springs (52126318AC heavy-duty front for Rubicon-spec ride height, 52126314AC for Sport/Sahara front, 77070091 rear) \u2014 these put you back to factory geometry. Cost is around $75\u2013$95 per spring. Second: aftermarket lift springs paired with longer shocks for 2\"\u20133\" of lift. Old Man Emu, Rough Country, Rubicon Express, and Teraflex all offer matched coil sets. Third: heavy-rate replacement coils to compensate for added weight (bumper, winch, half doors removed for permanent door storage) without adding lift.\n\nSprings always replace in pairs. Mixing a new spring with a tired one will give you a measurable lean. Replace fronts together, rears together \u2014 or all four at once if you're upgrading the build.\n\nFront install: support the frame on jack stands, disconnect the track bar at the axle, the sway bar end links, and one front shock per side. Drop the axle slowly with a floor jack \u2014 the front spring will lift out by hand once tension releases. Rear install: same approach but the rear sway bar disconnect is built into the geometry, and you'll need to disconnect the rear track bar. Watch the brake hoses and ABS lines as the axle drops.\n\nReassemble with the spring isolators (rubber pads at top and bottom) in place \u2014 missing isolators cause coil clunk over bumps. Torque shock bolts to 56 ft-lb, track bar bracket bolts to 125 ft-lb, sway bar end link to 75 ft-lb. Get an alignment after \u2014 caster changes as ride height changes.","steps":["Park on level ground. Loosen lug nuts on all four corners if doing all four springs.","Lift the frame and support on jack stands behind the front axle (front job) or in front of the rear axle (rear job).","Remove the wheels.","Disconnect the track bar at the axle side.","Disconnect the sway bar end links from the axle.","Disconnect one shock per side at the lower mount.","Lower the axle slowly with a floor jack until the coil spring releases \u2014 typically 6\"\u20138\" of droop.","Pull the spring out by hand. Inspect upper and lower isolators; replace if cracked.","Install the new spring with isolators seated correctly top and bottom.","Raise the axle back into position; reconnect shocks, sway bar, track bar.","Torque to spec: shock bolts 56 ft-lb, track bar 125 ft-lb, sway bar 75 ft-lb.","Lower the vehicle, settle the suspension by driving 5\u201310 miles, then re-check torque.","Schedule an alignment."],"pros":["Restores factory ride height and bump absorption","Doubles as a lift if you upgrade spring length","Springs are the cheapest suspension component to replace","No fluid bleed or special tooling needed"],"cons":["Track bar disconnection requires alignment after","Mismatched springs (new + old on same axle) cause visible lean","Aftermarket lift springs change caster \u2014 pair with adjustable control arms for 3\"+ lift","Heavier springs ride stiffer when the vehicle is empty"]},{"id":"jk-control-arm-replacement","title":"Control Arm Replacement and Upgrade","vehicle":"jk","category":"suspension","type":"task","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":200,"high":900},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","metric sockets 15mm-21mm","breaker bar","torque wrench (250 ft-lb capacity)","PB Blaster"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","lift-supported work","torque-to-yield procedure"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2007-2018"],"tags":["death-wobble","geometry","alignment"],"parts":[{"name":"Rough Country front adjustable LCAs (pair)","part_number":"11910","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":250},{"name":"Rubicon Express RE3751 front adjustable lower control arms","part_number":"RE3751","vendor":"Rubicon Express","price_estimate_usd":380},{"name":"Synergy HD adjustable rear lower control arms","vendor":"Synergy Manufacturing","price_estimate_usd":450},{"name":"Metalcloak Duroflex adjustable control arms","vendor":"Metalcloak","price_estimate_usd":600}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep Wrangler JK Suspension Torque Specs","url":"https://metalcloak.com/blog/jeep-wrangler-jk-suspension-torque-specs-the-complete-guide"},{"title":"Rubicon Express RE3751 Front Adjustable Lower Control Arms","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/products/16312_1118.htm"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Replace JK control arms when bushings are cracked, when you've lifted more than 2.5 inches and need to correct caster, or when you've bent one on a rock. Adjustable arms restore geometry; non-adjustable replacements only refresh worn bushings.","description":"The JK runs eight control arms \u2014 four front (two upper, two lower) and four rear (two upper, two lower). Each arm has a rubber bushing at one end and a flex joint at the other. When the bushings crack or the arm bends, ride quality degrades, alignment drifts, and on the front, you can trigger death wobble. The control arm is also the most common point at which lift kits fall short: cheap lifts often skip the arms entirely, leaving the axle pushed forward and caster yanked negative.\n\nFor a 2.5-inch or smaller lift, stock-length adjustable arms are usually enough. For 3.5 inches and up, you want adjustable arms in at least the front lowers \u2014 the axle shifts forward roughly 1/4 inch per inch of lift, and caster drops about a degree per inch. Adjustable lowers let you pull the axle back to roughly its factory position and reset caster to the 4\u20136 degree range the JK wants. Brand picks worth your money: Rubicon Express RE3751 (around $380, Currie-style joints), Synergy HD ($450/pair, weld-on grease zerks), Metalcloak Duroflex ($600, fully rebuildable). Rough Country adjustable LCAs at $250 work for a daily-driven mild build but use cheaper bushings that wear faster.\n\nTorque matters here and the factory uses torque-to-yield: front lower control arm front and rear nuts both spec 140 Nm + 145\u00b0 rotation (about 103 ft-lb then quarter-turn-plus). Front upper control arm front nut is 55 Nm + 185\u00b0. Rear lower front bolt is 100 Nm + 50\u00b0. Aftermarket arms often spec a flat ft-lb value instead \u2014 follow the manufacturer's number when present. The single biggest install mistake is torquing at full droop: do final torque at ride height, with the suspension loaded, so the bushings don't twist into preload and tear themselves apart in six months.\n\nIf you only have budget for two arms, do the front lowers first \u2014 they take the most abuse on the trail and they have the largest effect on caster.","steps":["Park on level ground, chock the rear wheels, and break front lug nuts loose before jacking.","Lift the front, support the frame on jack stands, and let the axle hang on the coils.","Soak both bolts on one arm with penetrant. Front lower arms run between the frame bracket and the axle bracket.","Remove the rear (frame-side) bolt first. Then the front (axle-side) nut. Pull the old arm out.","Measure the old arm center-to-center. Set the new adjustable arm to the same length as a starting point.","Install the new arm finger-tight. Repeat for the opposite side.","Lower the Jeep onto the ground or onto ramps so the suspension is at ride height.","Torque all bolts to spec with the suspension loaded.","Repeat for upper arms and for rear arms if replacing.","Get an alignment within 100 miles. Re-check caster and wheelbase against factory specs."],"pros":["Restores caster after a lift","HD construction resists bending on rocks","Fresh bushings or flex joints kill clunks and wobble","Adjustable arms let you fine-tune wheelbase"],"cons":["Front and rear sets together approach $1,500 at the high end","Flex joints can be noisy on pavement","Requires alignment afterward","Torque-to-yield bolts should be replaced, not reused, when factory hardware comes out"]},{"id":"jk-lift-kit-overview","title":"JK Lift Kit Types \u2014 Spacers vs. Short Arm vs. Long Arm","vehicle":"jk","category":"suspension","type":"swap","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":16},"cost_usd":{"low":150,"high":3500},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","metric sockets","torque wrench","spring compressor (sometimes)","pry bar"],"skills_required":["intermediate wrenching","geometry understanding","alignment basics"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2007-2010","2011-2018"],"tags":["lift","geometry","buying-guide"],"parts":[{"name":"Rough Country 2.5\" spacer lift","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":200},{"name":"Teraflex 2.5\" budget boost spacer","vendor":"Teraflex","price_estimate_usd":250},{"name":"Rough Country 3.5\" short-arm series II","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":900},{"name":"Metalcloak 3.5\" Game-Changer (short arm)","vendor":"Metalcloak","price_estimate_usd":2200},{"name":"Rock Krawler 3.5\" mid-arm X-Factor","vendor":"Rock Krawler","price_estimate_usd":1900},{"name":"EVO MFG 4.5\" Enforcer Stage 4 long arm","vendor":"EVO MFG","price_estimate_usd":3400}],"citations":[{"title":"Long Arm vs. Short Arm Lift Kits \u2014 JK-Forum","url":"https://www.jk-forum.com/forums/modified-jk-tech-2/long-arm-vs-short-arm-lift-kits-222079/"},{"title":"Choosing the Right Jeep Lift Kit \u2014 Dixie 4 Wheel Drive","url":"https://dixie4wheeldrive.com/choosing_right_jeep_lift_kit_long_vs_short_arm_lift_kits/"}],"alternatives":["jk-coil-spring-replacement","jk-control-arm-replacement","jk-shock-replacement"],"summary":"Spacers buy you height for cheap but no geometry correction; short-arm coil kits are the sweet spot for 2.5\"\u20133.5\" of usable lift; long-arm kits exist to fix the geometry problems you create when you go past 4\".","description":"The JK already comes from the factory with longer control arms than the XJ, TJ, or LJ, which means you can stack lift on it without immediately wrecking your geometry. That changes how you should think about lift options. A 2.5\" spacer on an XJ would be sketchy at best. On a JK, 2.5\" of spacer is a legitimate way to clear 33s without breaking the budget \u2014 provided you swap shocks at the same time.\n\nSpacer lifts (sometimes called \"budget boost\" or \"coil spacer\" kits) drop a puck on top of the coil spring. Cost is $150\u2013$300. You keep your stock coils, stock arms, stock track bar, stock everything. The Jeep sits taller. The trade-off is that you've stretched the shock travel without lengthening the shocks, so if you don't add longer shocks, full droop will tear the seals out of the OEM units within a few thousand miles. Rough Country and Teraflex both ship spacer kits with longer shocks for around $250.\n\nShort-arm coil kits replace the coils with taller springs and either keep or upgrade the stock control arms. Lift heights typically run 2.5\"\u20133.5\". This is where you start gaining real flex and ride quality. Brands worth looking at: Rough Country Series II (budget, $700\u2013$900), Teraflex (mid, $1500), Metalcloak Game-Changer (premium, $2000+), Rock Krawler X-Factor (mid-arm hybrid, $1900). At 3.5\" of short-arm lift, you'll want adjustable control arms to dial caster back in and a longer or adjustable track bar to re-center the axle.\n\nLong-arm kits replace the factory control-arm brackets entirely. You're cutting off the OEM brackets and welding new ones farther toward the center of the frame, which lets you run longer arms that flatten the arc the axle swings through. The benefit is genuine: on-road manners stay civil at 4.5\"+ of lift, bump steer drops, and you stop chewing through control-arm bushings. The cost is the install \u2014 these are $2500\u2013$4000 kits with welding and 12\u201316 hours of garage time. Brands: EVO MFG Enforcer, Rock Krawler Pro, Currie, Synergy. If you're not going past 4\" of lift, you're paying for capability you won't use.\n\nA practical rule: under 3.5\" of lift, short-arm is correct. At 4\" of lift, you're in the gray zone where mid-arm kits (Rock Krawler X-Factor, EVO Stage 2) start making sense. Past 4.5\" with 37s or 40s, long-arm is the only path that won't drive miserably and won't eat suspension parts.","steps":["Decide what tire size you actually want to run. The lift exists to clear the tire, not the other way around.","Match lift type to height: under 2.5\" spacer, 2.5\"\u20133.5\" short arm, 4\"+ mid or long arm.","Budget for the alignment, control arms, track bar correction, and drive shaft (long-arm kits usually need a slip-yoke eliminator or longer driveshaft).","Add longer shocks any time you spacer-lift \u2014 non-negotiable.","After install, set caster between 4\u00b0 and 6\u00b0 via adjustable control arms; get a real alignment.","Retorque all suspension fasteners after 100 trail miles. Bushings settle."],"pros":["Spacer lifts are cheap, reversible, and clear 33s on stock everything","Short-arm kits hit the value sweet spot for daily-trail rigs at 2.5\"\u20133.5\"","Long-arm kits keep ride quality and bushing life sane past 4.5\"","The JK's factory geometry tolerates moderate lift better than older platforms"],"cons":["Spacers without longer shocks will trash your OEM shocks","Short-arm past 3.5\" starts running into caster, drive shaft, and bump steer problems","Long-arm kits require welding and double the parts budget","Every lift category needs a fresh alignment \u2014 bake that into the cost"]},{"id":"jk-shock-replacement-jk","title":"Shock Absorber Replacement","vehicle":"jk","category":"suspension","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":220,"high":700},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","metric sockets","torque wrench","Allen key for shock shafts"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2007-2018"],"tags":["suspension","shocks","ride-quality"],"parts":[{"name":"Bilstein 5100 front shocks (1.5-3\" lift)","part_number":"24-146708","vendor":"Bilstein","price_estimate_usd":110},{"name":"Bilstein 5100 rear shocks (2\" lift)","part_number":"24-146715","vendor":"Bilstein","price_estimate_usd":110},{"name":"Fox 2.0 Performance Series IFP shocks","vendor":"Fox","price_estimate_usd":360},{"name":"Old Man Emu Nitrocharger Sport","vendor":"ARB / OME","price_estimate_usd":280}],"citations":[{"title":"JK Bilstein 5100 install (ExtremeTerrain)","url":"https://www.extremeterrain.com/bilstein-5100-re-shock-2-35-jk-24146715-cust-install.html"},{"title":"JK Suspension Torque Specs","url":"https://www.extremeterrain.com/wrangler-jk-suspension-torque-specs.html"},{"title":"Bilstein 5100 Shock Set 2007-2018 JK","url":"https://www.shocksurplus.com/products/bilstein-5100-monotube-shocks-set-for-2007-2018-jeep-wrangler-jk-4wd"}],"alternatives":["jk-lift-kit-overview-jk","jk-coil-spring-replacement-jk"],"summary":"Worn shocks let the JK porpoise on freeway expansion joints, wallow in corners, and bounce after bumps; replacing all four is a two-hour driveway job that completely changes how the truck feels.","description":"Shocks wear out by 60,000-80,000 miles even on stock JKs, and lifted JKs eat them faster because the stock shock travel is wrong for the new ride height. Symptoms of dead shocks: bounce more than one cycle after a speed bump, front-end dive under braking, tail squat under acceleration, and a \"floaty\" feel on the highway. Pull a shock and shake it \u2014 if there's any free play in either direction without resistance, it's done.\n\nFor a stock-height JK, replacement OE-spec shocks (Bilstein B6 or Mopar) run $50-$80 each and are fine. For a 2-3.5\" lift, the Bilstein 5100 (front 24-146708, rear 24-146715) is the value benchmark at roughly $440 for the set \u2014 monotube design, zinc-plated, with the right extended length for a typical mid-height JK. Fox 2.0 IFPs ($90-100 each) ride better over washboard at the cost of more dust seal wear. Old Man Emu Nitrochargers ride softer than Bilsteins, better for overlanders carrying weight.\n\nSkip \"lifetime warranty\" parts-store shocks. They're rebadged budget twin-tubes that fade after a hot trail run and feel mushy when new.\n\nInstall is bolt-in. The only annoying part is the front lower shock bolt, which often seizes \u2014 soak it in penetrating oil the night before. The shock shaft will spin while you tighten the upper nut; use an Allen key socket on the shaft top to hold it steady.\n\nTorque specs after install: front upper 20 ft-lb, front lower 56 ft-lb, rear upper 37 ft-lb, rear lower 65 ft-lb. Bounce-test each corner before lowering \u2014 a properly seated shock returns to ride height with one cycle, not two.","steps":["Park on level ground, chock the wheels you aren't lifting, and break loose the lug nuts before jacking.","Jack one corner and set on a stand. Remove the wheel for working clearance.","Front: remove the upper nut (hold the shaft with an Allen key), then the lower bolt at the axle bracket. Compress the old shock and pull down/out.","Compare new and old shock length \u2014 they should match when fully extended.","Install upper end first, then lower. Hand-thread both before torquing.","Torque: front upper 20 ft-lb, front lower 56 ft-lb, rear upper 37 ft-lb, rear lower 65 ft-lb.","Repeat at remaining three corners.","Lower the vehicle, re-torque lug nuts to 95 ft-lb, and bounce-test each corner."],"pros":["Restores ride quality and handling","Bolt-in, no alignment needed","Bilstein 5100 set is under $500 for all four","Improves towing and load stability"],"cons":["Lower bolts often seize and require penetrating oil or heat","Wrong-length shocks for your lift height will top out or bottom out","Cheap shocks fade fast \u2014 buy once, cry once"]},{"id":"jk-sway-bar-disconnect-jk","title":"Electric Sway Bar Disconnect \u2014 Troubleshooting","vehicle":"jk","category":"suspension","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":650},"tools_required":["dielectric grease","T20 Torx","metric sockets","multimeter","marine grease","wire brush"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","electrical inspection"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2007-2018"],"tags":["rubicon","smartbar","electrical","water-intrusion"],"parts":[{"name":"EVO Manufacturing No Limits Manual Disconnect","vendor":"EVO MFG","price_estimate_usd":350},{"name":"EVO No-Limits On Demand (air actuated)","vendor":"EVO MFG","price_estimate_usd":650},{"name":"Mopar sway bar actuator motor","part_number":"68210857AB","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":320},{"name":"Marine-grade grease (tub)","vendor":"Lucas / Sta-Lube","price_estimate_usd":12}],"citations":[{"title":"Sway Bar Issues \u2014 Native Jeeps","url":"https://jeeptourscolorado.com/jeep-blog/sway-bar/"},{"title":"Rubicon Manual Sway Bar Disconnects \u2014 JK-Forum","url":"https://www.jk-forum.com/articles/rubicon-manual-sway-bar-disconnects/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Most JK Rubicon sway bar disconnect failures trace to water in the connector and grease washed out of the actuator \u2014 clean, re-grease, and seal the harness before you spend $300 on a new motor.","description":"The American Axle SmartBar that came standard on every Rubicon JK is a clever piece of engineering and a notorious headache. An electric actuator pushes a shift fork across splines to connect or disconnect the front sway bar. The mechanical side is robust. The electrical side \u2014 a CAN-bus connected actuator riding under the front axle right where water and mud live \u2014 is the problem.\n\nThe failure pattern is consistent. The dash light flashes faster than normal or stays solid. Sometimes the sway bar refuses to reconnect on the highway, which the PCM will not allow above ~18 mph. Sometimes the dash throws other CAN errors that look unrelated \u2014 locker buttons go dead, the steering wheel controls quit, even check-engine lights \u2014 because the shorted e-disco is corrupting the CAN bus. Before condemning the actuator, check the simple stuff first.\n\nStart with the connector. Disconnect the harness from the sway bar, look for green corrosion or water inside, and clean it out with electrical contact cleaner. Pack the pins with dielectric grease and reseal. Then check the actuator itself: pull the four T20 screws holding the motor housing, peek inside for water and rust on the gears. If you find dry rusty gears, the grease is gone. Clean the assembly, repack with marine-grade grease, and reassemble. That repair alone fixes a high share of e-disco complaints, and the only cost is grease and an afternoon.\n\nIf the motor genuinely no longer cycles, a Mopar replacement actuator runs about $320. At that price point, most owners take a hard look at swapping to a manual disconnect setup. The EVO No Limits Manual Disconnect at roughly $350 replaces the entire sway bar with a fixed bar plus a pin-style disconnect \u2014 bombproof, no electronics, but you have to get out and pull a pin. The EVO No-Limits On Demand at roughly $650 is air-actuated from the cab, which keeps the convenience without the CAN-bus fragility.","steps":["Park on level ground, chock the rear wheels, and lower the front to a stable height. You need access to the front sway bar.","Unplug the harness connector at the actuator. Inspect for moisture, corrosion, and bent pins.","Spray the connector and pins with electrical contact cleaner, dry thoroughly, then pack with dielectric grease and reseat.","Remove the four T20 screws from the actuator motor cover. Inspect the gear assembly inside for water, rust, or dried grease.","Clean any rust from the gears with a wire brush, repack with marine-grade grease, and reassemble. Torque the cover screws snug \u2014 they thread into plastic, do not overtighten.","Reconnect, cycle the disconnect button several times, and verify the dash light goes solid (connected) and off (disconnected) without flashing rapidly.","If the symptoms return, replace the actuator motor or convert to a manual or air disconnect."],"pros":["Restores stock disconnect function for the cost of grease","Catches the CAN-bus side effects (dead locker buttons, dead steering wheel controls)","Annual cleaning prevents most failures","Manual or air conversion eliminates the failure mode entirely"],"cons":["Same problem will recur if the connector is not properly sealed","Mopar replacement actuator is $300+","Manual conversion means stopping and getting out at the trailhead","Air conversion adds cost and a compressor requirement"]}];